{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2180-09-23 22:11:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I'm Dr. Lee, one of the hospitalists. I’ll be helping care for you during your stay. You were admitted because of high potassium, is that right?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2180-09-23 22:13:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, that’s what they told me. I’ve had this before—felt weak in my legs, shaky. It’s scary."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2180-09-23 22:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I understand. Your potassium was very high—7.6—so we gave you some treatment in the ER to protect your heart and then started dialysis."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2180-09-23 22:17:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I remember getting insulin and sugar. I felt worse for a bit—my blood sugar dropped, didn’t it?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2180-09-23 22:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, your sugar went low after the insulin, down to the 20s. We gave you dextrose and juice, and it came back up. You’re stable now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2180-09-23 22:21:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Good. I’ve been doing all my dialysis, taking my kayexalate. I don’t know why this happened."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2180-09-23 22:23:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s what we’re trying to figure out. You’ve had normal diets and no missed treatments. One clue is your INR was low—1.4—when it should be between 1.5 and 2.5."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2180-09-23 22:25:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m on warfarin for my antiphospholipid syndrome. My dose is a bit confusing—3 and 4 mg on different days."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2180-09-23 22:27:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, and your nephrologist thinks the low INR might be linked to the high potassium. We’ll adjust your warfarin to 5 mg daily to keep it in range."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2180-09-23 22:29:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, I can do that. But I also had belly pain earlier. Is that gone for good?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2180-09-23 22:31:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, your abdominal pain has resolved. We did a CT scan to check for serious causes like bowel ischemia, but everything looked okay."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2180-09-23 22:33:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And the little blood on the toilet paper? Should I worry?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2180-09-23 22:35:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Very minimal, and no other signs of bleeding. With your history of hysterectomy, it’s likely from the rectal area. We’ll keep an eye on it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2180-09-23 22:37:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Alright. I’ve also been on levofloxacin for sinusitis. They stopped it in the ER, right?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2180-09-23 22:39:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, we held it because of your low blood sugar, but now that you’re stable, we’ll restart it tomorrow to finish your course."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2180-09-23 22:41:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Good. I still have a headache sometimes."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2180-09-24 08:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your labs are improving. Potassium is down to 5.2 after dialysis, which is much better."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2180-09-24 08:17:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s a relief. But my left arm feels worse—more swollen and cool to the touch."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2180-09-24 08:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We noticed that. Your left arm has a fistula, and there’s a lot of swelling and some bluish fingers. We’re concerned about poor blood return."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2180-09-24 08:21:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve had swelling before. Is it a clot?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2180-09-24 08:23:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We did an ultrasound last night—no clot found. But there’s stenosis in the vein and lots of collateral veins, which can cause this."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2180-09-24 08:25:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So what do we do?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2180-09-24 08:27:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Transplant surgery evaluated you. They recommend eventually ligating the left fistula since it’s causing the swelling."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2180-09-24 08:29:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Ligate it? So stop using it?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2180-09-24 08:31:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes. You have a right arm fistula too, so we can use that. The left one is causing too much strain and poor circulation."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2180-09-24 08:33:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, if it’s necessary. But I should keep my arm elevated now?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2180-09-24 08:35:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. Try to keep it above heart level when you can. That helps reduce swelling."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2180-09-24 14:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We got your CT results back. No bleeding or bowel issues. Also saw some small cysts in your pancreas, but they’re likely harmless."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2180-09-24 14:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And the calcified mass in my pelvis?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2180-09-24 14:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s your old transplant kidney. It’s inactive and calcified, which is expected since it failed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2180-09-25 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your labs are stable. Hemoglobin dropped a bit, but it’s consistent with your chronic kidney disease. No sign of bleeding."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2180-09-25 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I feel tired. Is that from the anemia?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2180-09-25 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Likely. Your EPO dose was recently increased, so that should help over time. We’ll keep giving it with dialysis."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2180-09-25 09:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my blood pressure? I wasn’t sure what to take—labetalol or amlodipine."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2180-09-25 09:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We held your blood pressure meds at first because of dialysis. Now you’re on amlodipine, and your pressures are good."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2180-09-25 09:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Should I restart labetalol?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2180-09-25 09:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not right now. You’re doing well without it. Check with your PCP before restarting."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2180-09-26 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re doing well. We’re planning to discharge you today. Your potassium is stable, and your arm swelling is being managed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2180-09-26 10:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Great. What do I need to do at home?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2180-09-26 10:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Continue your low-potassium diet, take warfarin 5 mg daily, and finish your levofloxacin. Keep your left arm elevated."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2180-09-26 10:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And the INR check?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2180-09-26 10:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, get your INR and basic labs checked on your next dialysis days. Fax the results to your nephrologist."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2180-09-26 10:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And follow-up with transplant surgery?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2180-09-26 10:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. They’ll schedule the fistula ligation. Call us if your weight goes up more than 3 pounds or arm swelling worsens."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2180-09-26 10:14:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you, Doctor. I feel much better."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2180-09-26 10:16:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. We’ll get your discharge papers ready. Take care."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 47,
      "time": "2180-09-26 10:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "One more thing—my CPAP. I didn’t use it here."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 48,
      "time": "2180-09-26 10:20:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I know. You refused it, but please use it at home, especially with your oxygen needs."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 49,
      "time": "2180-09-26 10:22:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ll try. The mask bothers me, but I’ll make an effort."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 50,
      "time": "2180-09-26 10:24:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s all we ask. Small steps. Let the nurse know when you’re ready to go."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2180-09-23 22:11:00",
    "admission_end": "2180-09-26 20:14:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A female patient with end-stage renal disease and a history of multiple comorbidities, including antiphospholipid syndrome and lupus, was admitted for symptomatic hyperkalemia with muscle weakness. She was treated with intravenous calcium, insulin, dextrose, and urgent hemodialysis, which normalized her potassium levels. During the hospitalization, her subtherapeutic INR of 1.4 was identified, and warfarin was increased to 5 mg daily. She developed worsening left arm edema, which ultrasound showed was not due to deep vein thrombosis; transplant surgery recommended future ligation of the left arm fistula due to venous stenosis and collateralization. Abdominal pain and mild rectal bleeding resolved without intervention, and a CT scan showed no acute pathology. She completed a course of levofloxacin for sinusitis and was discharged home in stable condition with close follow-up planned for anticoagulation monitoring and fistula management.",
    "problems": [
      "Hyperpotassemia",
      "End stage renal disease",
      "Subtherapeutic INR",
      "Edema of left arm due to brachiocephalic and subclavian vein stenosis and increased venous collaterals",
      "Anemia in chronic kidney disease",
      "Hypertensive chronic kidney disease, malignant, with chronic kidney disease stage V or end stage renal disease",
      "Systemic lupus erythematosus",
      "Antiphospholipid antibody syndrome",
      "Obstructive sleep apnea (adult)(pediatric)",
      "Depressive disorder, not elsewhere classified",
      "Anxiety state, unspecified"
    ]
  }
}
